WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.799
So last of all, we've already talked about Blueprint a little bit at the beginning of the presentation,

2
00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:08.990
but it's worth circling back round to it to finish off.

3
00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:15.990
I've already mentioned this, but Blueprint is an extensive suite of tools built on top of a C++ Foundation.

4
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:21.989
It allows developers, creatives, artists, designers to get stuck in and start creating things immediately.

5
00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:25.989
It's built directly into the editor, meaning it can empower any member of the team,

6
00:00:25.989 --> 00:00:31.989
and they can just start developing their ideas with significantly reduced iteration time.

7
00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:37.990
It's great for testing out ideas and debugging them really quickly in very visual ways.

8
00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:45.990
But something that's worth mentioning is that a well-structured Blueprint can and should be considered production-ready code.

9
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:53.990
Blueprint is not just for experimentation. It's not just the kind of thing that is less important than C++.

10
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:59.990
It should very much be considered that a fully-featured Blueprint is shipping-ready.

11
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.490
These two things are not distinct from each other.

12
00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:08.489
In fact, the vast majority of Unreal games are developed with these two things in tandem,

13
00:01:08.489 --> 00:01:12.280
and they both have advantages and disadvantages.

14
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.280
That being said, when you're developing a Blueprint, you should be making the same considerations

15
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.280
that you would make whenever you're working in C++.

16
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.780
If this isn't just iteration and exploration, and we're going for a fully-featured system,

17
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.780
we do need to be considering the same things.

18
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.780
Are the functions I'm running in this going to be killing my frame rate?

19
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.780
Am I doing this thing more frequently than I need to be doing it?

20
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:38.780
Is this methodology or functionality that I'm using as efficient as it could be?

21
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.780
Have I made good use of object-oriented principles when developing this Blueprint?

22
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.780
Am I jumping all over the place?

23
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.780
The same considerations need to be taken into account.

24
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.780
That being said, that's not to say there is no room for C++.

25
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.780
These two things are meant to communicate.

26
00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.780
So how do we do that? How do we communicate between C++ and Blueprint?

27
00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.780
We've already touched on this a little bit, but it's worth going back over it.

28
00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.780
Ultimately, Blueprint is built on top of C++.

29
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:11.780
The way we expose these two things to each other, the way we expose C++ to Blueprint,

30
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:18.780
is through a set of markup that we can do via UProperties, UFunctions, and things like that.

31
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:24.780
Very, very common examples of this would be with UFunction, we have BlueprintCallable

32
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.780
and a BlueprintCallable function that is const or marked as BlueprintPure.

33
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:33.780
A BlueprintCallable function is a function that is intended to be called via Blueprint.

34
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.780
So it's C++ code that will be executed, but Blueprint can see it and call it.

35
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.780
An example of that would be setActorLocation on the Actor class.

36
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:49.780
A BlueprintCallable with const or BlueprintPure function works in the same way,

37
00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:53.780
but it looks and executes slightly differently in Blueprint.

38
00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.780
So that would be getActorLocation.

39
00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:01.780
These are both BlueprintCallable in a sense functions, but getActorLocation is BlueprintPure.

40
00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:07.780
The distinction, which is relatively obvious, being that BlueprintPure functions don't have this execution line.

41
00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:12.780
Whereas setActorLocation will get executed as and when this execution line is followed here

42
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.780
and we hit this pin, getActorLocation is called whenever its return value

43
00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.780
or one of its return values, if it has multiple, is referenced.

44
00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.780
The important distinction here, and this is very important to bear in mind,

45
00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.780
is that a normal BlueprintCallable function will cache all its output values.

46
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:34.780
So at the point that we execute this, it takes all its inputs and we cache our sweep hit result

47
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.780
and our return value here.

48
00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.840
And any subsequent functions that use that data,

49
00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.840
there will just be a cached value for each of these pins.

50
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.840
And unlike that, when we try and use getActorLocation,

51
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.840
BlueprintPure functions rerun the function every time that value is accessed.

52
00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.840
So when we have these two print strings, because we get the output from getActorLocation twice,

53
00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.840
getActorLocation will be run twice.

54
00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:10.840
So whereas with getActorLocation it's relatively cheap, with more expensive functions,

55
00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.840
it can be something very important to bear in mind that when you're doing something pure,

56
00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.839
it will be rerun every time we try and use one of its outputs.

57
00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:19.839
So it's a very important distinction.

58
00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:24.000
But the advantage you gain is obviously it can be good for tidiness's sake,

59
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.000
it can be good for kind of speed's sake,

60
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.000
and sometimes you don't want everything on your execution path

61
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.000
that can make your Blueprints very, very long when they don't necessarily need to be.

62
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:42.089
Another two bits of UFunction markup that are very important to bear in mind

63
00:04:42.089 --> 00:04:46.089
and you'll be using a lot are BlueprintImplementableEvent and BlueprintNativeEvent.

64
00:04:46.089 --> 00:04:51.089
BlueprintImplementableEventss are essentially a little bit like notifies.

65
00:04:51.089 --> 00:04:56.089
If we have some, for example, a cosmetic functionality that we want to trigger from code,

66
00:04:56.089 --> 00:05:01.089
but we just kind of want to notify Blueprint that it has happened,

67
00:05:01.089 --> 00:05:03.089
we can do that with a BlueprintImplementableEvent,

68
00:05:03.089 --> 00:05:08.089
and then any Blueprint inheriting from that or has access to that can be notified of that.

69
00:05:08.089 --> 00:05:12.089
BlueprintNativeEvent is very similar to BlueprintImplementable,

70
00:05:12.089 --> 00:05:17.089
but the idea being that there is also a C++ implementation of this function.

71
00:05:17.089 --> 00:05:22.089
So the way this will work is it will look for the most inherited class,

72
00:05:22.089 --> 00:05:28.089
and if the Blueprint implements that event, then it will use that one,

73
00:05:28.089 --> 00:05:33.089
but if there is no Blueprint implementation, it will default back to a C++ implementation.

74
00:05:33.089 --> 00:05:37.540
So very, very similar, but BlueprintNative has C++.

75
00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:40.540
BlueprintImplementable is Blueprint only.

76
00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:45.540
It's worth highlighting as well that with BlueprintImplementableEventss,

77
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:52.540
even though your compiler or your IDE might complain that this is expecting a body,

78
00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:56.540
actually it's not. Unreal will expect for BlueprintImplementableEventss

79
00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:00.540
there to be no body inside your C++ code.

80
00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:03.540
And this is just an example of a BlueprintImplementableEvent

81
00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.540
that's very, very common in the editor itself, is we have BeginPlay.

82
00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:12.540
Very much like normal virtual functions in C++,

83
00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.540
BlueprintImplementableEvents and BlueprintNativeEvents,

84
00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:23.540
the most inherited form, the lowest implementation in a class hierarchy of that function

85
00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:27.540
will be the one that gets called, is then up to that function

86
00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:31.540
to call the parent implementations if that's desired.

87
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:36.540
So with your BeginPlay, if I have a Blueprint class that inherits from a Blueprint class,

88
00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:41.540
inherits from a Blueprint class, it is the bottommost child of that inheritance hierarchy

89
00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.540
that will have its BeginPlay called.

90
00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:47.540
If you want the parents to have their BeginPlay BlueprintImplementableEventss called,

91
00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.540
then you need to call this parent BeginPlay,

92
00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:52.540
and the same thing is true for BlueprintNativeEvents.

93
00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:55.540
And that goes all the way back up to C++.

94
00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:59.139
On the other side, with UProperties,

95
00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:02.139
some examples of typical things that you might need to do.

96
00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:07.139
As a programmer, we internally use C++ and Blueprints ourselves,

97
00:07:07.139 --> 00:07:11.139
but a lot of the time it's about exposing functionality to design,

98
00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:18.139
but also about trying to ensure that design do things in the way that you intend.

99
00:07:18.139 --> 00:07:22.139
Very common uses of that might be UIMin and UIMax,

100
00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:26.139
so that people can't put in ridiculous values that you don't want them to be able to use.

101
00:07:26.139 --> 00:07:30.139
So if a value should only ever be between 0 and 10,

102
00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:35.139
you can give it a visual that essentially gives you this kind of slider functionality,

103
00:07:35.139 --> 00:07:39.139
and you can also clamp those values so that it literally will lock a value

104
00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:45.139
so that it doesn't go higher or lower than two numbers that you specify.

105
00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:49.360
Another thing that you might commonly want to do is use display names

106
00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:56.360
so that some internal massive name can be condensed for readability

107
00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.360
on the actual Unreal editor side of things.

108
00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.360
And last of all, you can specify getters and setters.

109
00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.360
So for example, if we have some kind of value,

110
00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.360
but we want to inject some functionality on top,

111
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.360
this might be common for people who are used to writing C sharp,

112
00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.360
where getters and setters are very common.

113
00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.360
You can basically specify a function to be run

114
00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.360
whenever we try and get this value,

115
00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.360
and a function to be run whenever we try and set this value.

116
00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:25.360
And those would look exactly like the normal get and set nodes,

117
00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.360
but actually secretly under the hood, it's calling these functions.

118
00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.360
So those are kind of things that you might commonly use,

119
00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.360
but that being said, both of these things, UFunction and UProperty,

120
00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:38.360
have far, far more extensive lists of things that you can do

121
00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.360
and things that you can utilize in the objectmacros.h,

122
00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.970
so it's worth looking up all of those.

123
00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.970
Last of all, it's worth highlighting that

124
00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.970
because Blueprint is a fully featured scripting language,

125
00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.970
it also implements interfaces.

126
00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.970
So the way we would introduce an interface at C++ level,

127
00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:01.970
they can also be created in Blueprint,

128
00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.970
there are Blueprint interfaces, but at C++ level,

129
00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:06.970
is we would create an interface class,

130
00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:08.970
and that would look something like this.

131
00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:10.970
So if you create one through Unreal,

132
00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:12.970
it will automatically generate a class that looks like this.

133
00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:14.970
We have the U interface section of the class

134
00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.970
and the I interface part of the class.

135
00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:21.970
U interface is pretty much there for interaction with Blueprint,

136
00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:24.970
so there's very little you need to do here,

137
00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:27.970
there is just whatever you need to specify

138
00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:31.970
in this U interface markup here in the macro at the top.

139
00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:35.970
So for example, if you want this to be accessible by Blueprint,

140
00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:41.320
you would need to add the Blueprintable markup.

141
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:44.320
And then any functions that you want to implement inside this interface

142
00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:48.320
would then go in the I interface part of the interface.

143
00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:50.320
And then each of these functions,

144
00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:54.320
as long as this is part of a U interface class,

145
00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:58.320
as long as it's a class that has that matching U interface class,

146
00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.320
each of these functions can then have normal U function markup above it,

147
00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:07.320
and all of the same U function properties will be available to you,

148
00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.320
anything that you can do to a normal class U function you can do here.

149
00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.320
So for example, this could be a Blueprint implementable event,

150
00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.320
it could be a Blueprint native event, we can change the display name,

151
00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.149
we can do any kind of meta markup there that we want to do.

152
00:10:20.149 --> 00:10:24.149
It's worth highlighting as well that not all functions

153
00:10:24.149 --> 00:10:26.149
inside a Blueprint interface

154
00:10:26.149 --> 00:10:29.149
have to be Blueprint callable or exposed to Blueprint.

155
00:10:29.149 --> 00:10:32.149
Some of them can be specifically for native,

156
00:10:32.149 --> 00:10:34.149
and that's an important distinction to bear in mind

157
00:10:34.149 --> 00:10:37.149
when we're talking about actually using that interface.

158
00:10:37.149 --> 00:10:39.149
So the way we might utilize that in code

159
00:10:39.149 --> 00:10:43.149
once we have something that implements that interface is,

160
00:10:43.149 --> 00:10:45.149
well, there's really two ways.

161
00:10:45.149 --> 00:10:48.149
So we can call this templated function implements,

162
00:10:48.149 --> 00:10:52.149
so something that implements this interface,

163
00:10:52.149 --> 00:10:55.149
we can check that through this templated function.

164
00:10:55.149 --> 00:10:59.149
Here we do need to pass in the U interface type,

165
00:10:59.149 --> 00:11:02.149
so the I1 will cause a compile issue,

166
00:11:02.149 --> 00:11:04.149
we need to specify the U interface type,

167
00:11:04.149 --> 00:11:06.149
and then that would have turned true

168
00:11:06.149 --> 00:11:10.379
if this object does implement that interface.

169
00:11:10.379 --> 00:11:13.379
If it does, and then we want to call functions on that,

170
00:11:13.379 --> 00:11:16.379
with the U interface and the I interface,

171
00:11:16.379 --> 00:11:19.379
a set of functions will be statically generated

172
00:11:19.379 --> 00:11:22.379
that you then statically call

173
00:11:22.379 --> 00:11:24.379
through the I version of the interface.

174
00:11:24.379 --> 00:11:27.379
So I my interface, and as you can see,

175
00:11:27.379 --> 00:11:30.379
we had this some Blueprint implementable function,

176
00:11:30.379 --> 00:11:34.379
we call execute, we get the static version of the interface,

177
00:11:34.379 --> 00:11:37.379
execute underscore and then the function name.

178
00:11:37.379 --> 00:11:42.379
Because this function takes two parameters,

179
00:11:42.379 --> 00:11:44.379
we can see those are passed in here and here,

180
00:11:44.379 --> 00:11:48.379
the first parameter of this static version of the function

181
00:11:48.379 --> 00:11:50.379
takes in the actual object that you're calling it on.

182
00:11:50.379 --> 00:11:52.379
So as you can see, because it's static,

183
00:11:52.379 --> 00:11:54.379
there is no concept of the object we're calling it on,

184
00:11:54.379 --> 00:11:59.379
so the first parameter is which object am I calling this function on.

185
00:11:59.379 --> 00:12:02.379
The alternative and the kind of more,

186
00:12:02.379 --> 00:12:06.379
the way that you might assume to do it in C++ more naturally

187
00:12:06.379 --> 00:12:12.379
is to just cast that object using the I version of the interface

188
00:12:12.379 --> 00:12:14.379
to check whether or not that interface,

189
00:12:14.379 --> 00:12:16.379
whether or not it's null, and if it's not null,

190
00:12:16.379 --> 00:12:19.379
we would go inside this if statement body.

191
00:12:19.379 --> 00:12:22.379
That's absolutely fine, but it's worth bearing in mind,

192
00:12:22.379 --> 00:12:24.379
and very important to bear in mind,

193
00:12:24.379 --> 00:12:28.379
that when you cast to an interface in this way,

194
00:12:28.379 --> 00:12:30.379
that will tell you whether or not

195
00:12:30.379 --> 00:12:32.379
that object implements that interface,

196
00:12:32.379 --> 00:12:36.379
but if you have functions that are implemented in Blueprint,

197
00:12:36.379 --> 00:12:39.379
those will not get detected when you try and call them,

198
00:12:39.379 --> 00:12:43.379
and that can lead to a lot of very frustrating and slow debugging.

199
00:12:43.379 --> 00:12:48.379
Native functions and natively implemented functions

200
00:12:48.379 --> 00:12:51.379
for that interface will work absolutely fine,

201
00:12:51.379 --> 00:12:54.379
but if you have functions that are either Blueprint implementable

202
00:12:54.379 --> 00:12:58.379
or Blueprint native, it simply won't see those.

203
00:12:58.379 --> 00:13:00.379
So if you had a Blueprint native function,

204
00:13:00.379 --> 00:13:03.379
it would probably call the C++ version of that function,

205
00:13:03.379 --> 00:13:05.379
but even if you're calling on a Blueprint

206
00:13:05.379 --> 00:13:07.379
that overrides that functionality,

207
00:13:07.379 --> 00:13:10.379
it would probably just call the C++ native version of the function

208
00:13:10.379 --> 00:13:13.379
and not the Blueprint implementation.

209
00:13:13.379 --> 00:13:16.379
So if you have anything that is Blueprint implementable

210
00:13:16.379 --> 00:13:19.379
or Blueprint native, unfortunately,

211
00:13:19.379 --> 00:13:23.379
using this casting to the interface is not going to be suitable.

212
00:13:23.379 --> 00:13:28.169
In short, Blueprint is an incredible tool

213
00:13:28.169 --> 00:13:30.169
and should be used to its fullest extent,

214
00:13:30.169 --> 00:13:32.169
and it should be very much considered

215
00:13:32.169 --> 00:13:35.169
as something that is in tandem with C++.

216
00:13:35.169 --> 00:13:37.169
We use both of these things together,

217
00:13:37.169 --> 00:13:39.169
and they both have their advantages.

218
00:13:39.169 --> 00:13:41.169
Again, incredibly fast iteration times,

219
00:13:41.169 --> 00:13:43.169
visual debugability with Blueprints,

220
00:13:43.169 --> 00:13:47.169
it can sometimes actually be much easier to see execution paths in real time

221
00:13:47.169 --> 00:13:50.169
rather than having to stop everything with breakpoints

222
00:13:50.169 --> 00:13:51.169
and step through your code.

223
00:13:51.169 --> 00:13:54.169
You can watch it all happening as it's running.

224
00:13:54.169 --> 00:13:57.169
Where C++, slightly more performant,

225
00:13:57.169 --> 00:14:01.169
access to the full range of all the Unreal Engine code base

226
00:14:01.169 --> 00:14:03.169
and increased debugability

227
00:14:03.169 --> 00:14:05.169
when you get into the fine-grained things

228
00:14:05.169 --> 00:14:08.169
like really needing to see every variable

229
00:14:08.169 --> 00:14:10.169
and things like your call stack.

230
00:14:10.169 --> 00:14:14.860
So wrapping up, we're just going to talk a little bit

231
00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.860
about some of the resources that we've got available to us.

232
00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:20.860
Obviously, this has been a bit of a whirlwind exploration

233
00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.860
of a lot of the basic fundamentals.

234
00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:25.860
If you want to follow up on any of this,

235
00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:28.860
we have Unreal Engine documentation at unrealengine.com.

236
00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:30.860
We have the Unreal Engine YouTube channel.

237
00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:31.860
We have a Twitch.

238
00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:34.860
UDN is the Unreal Developer Network.

239
00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:37.860
This is dedicated for Unreal Engine licensees,

240
00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.860
so you would need to be a licensee to have access to this.

241
00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.860
But if that's not available to you,

242
00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:45.860
then we do still have the AnswerHub,

243
00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:51.860
which is essentially a little bit like Quora or Yahoo Answers,

244
00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:53.860
but specifically around Unreal.

245
00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:55.860
Very, very active community there,

246
00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:58.860
really good for looking for people

247
00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:00.860
who might have already stumbled across the same issue as you.

248
00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:03.860
And then last of all, we have the Unreal Engine Community Wiki,

249
00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:07.860
where people have hosted up a whole load of different kinds of tutorials

250
00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.860
that have come more from the community side

251
00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:16.139
than official Epic stuff.

252
00:15:16.139 --> 00:15:17.139
So I think that's everything.

253
00:15:17.139 --> 00:15:20.139
It's a lot to cover, and it's a lot to take in.

254
00:15:20.139 --> 00:15:24.139
Like I said, this has been a very, very shallow view

255
00:15:24.139 --> 00:15:25.139
of all of these things,

256
00:15:25.139 --> 00:15:29.139
but it's just good to have this kind of broad understanding

257
00:15:29.139 --> 00:15:32.139
of a lot of these different systems and functionalities

258
00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:34.139
when you're coming to Unreal for the first time.

259
00:15:34.139 --> 00:15:36.139
Maybe not all of this sticks the first time,

260
00:15:36.139 --> 00:15:38.139
might need a couple of rewatches,

261
00:15:38.139 --> 00:15:41.139
but hopefully we've just planted enough seeds

262
00:15:41.139 --> 00:15:43.139
that next time you kind of come across something,

263
00:15:43.139 --> 00:15:45.139
you might be like, oh yeah, I think I remember something about that.

264
00:15:45.139 --> 00:15:47.139
Hopefully this has been useful.

265
00:15:47.139 --> 00:15:49.139
Thanks very much for watching, and see you next time.
